2. [''l. c.''] A sandpiper, or some similar small wader.—'''Coot-footed tringa''', {{smaller|a cootfoot. See cut under ''phalarope''. ''Edwards''.}}

'''Tringeæ''' (trin′jē-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Tringa'' + ''-eæ''.] The true sandpipers, as a section of the subfamily ''Scolopacinæ''. See cuts under ''dunlin'', ''sanderling'', ''sandpiper'', and ''stint''. ''Coues'', 1861.

'''Tringidæ†''' (trin′ji-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Tringa'' + ''-idæ''.] The sandpipers regarded as a family apart from ''Scolopacidæ''.

'''Tringinæ''' (trin-jī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Tringa'' + ''-inæ''.] The sandpipers as a subfamily of ''Scolopacidæ''.

'''tringine''' (trin′jin), ''a.'' [< ''Tringa'' + ''-ine''<sup>1</sup>.] Having the character of a sandpiper; belonging to the ''Tringinæ'' or ''Tringeæ'': distinguished from ''scolopacine'' and ''totanine''.

