'''tringoid''' (tring′goid), ''a.'' [< ''Tringa'' + Gr. εἶδος, form.] Resembling the genus ''Tringa''; like a sandpiper. The ''Thinocoridæ'' have been singularly called ''tringoid grouse.''

'''Tringoides''' (tring-goi′dēz), ''n.'' [NL. (Bonaparte, 1831), < ''Tringa'' + Gr. εἶδος, form.] A genus of small tattlers; the spotted sandpipers. Also called ''Actitis.'' {{smaller|The common sandpiper of Europe, etc., is ''T. hypoleucus''; the spotted sandpiper of America, ''T. macularius.'' The latter is 7 or 8 inches long; the upper}}

