{{smaller|parts are Quaker-color, finely marked with black; the under parts are white, crowded with round black spots; the bill is pale-yellow, tipped with black, and the feet are flesh-colored. This sandpiper abounds in suitable places throughout the United States, breeds at large in its North American range, and lays four eggs in a slight nest on the ground. It is familiarly known as the ''sand-lark'', ''peetweet'' (from its cry), and ''teetertail'', ''tilt-up'', ''tip-up'', from its habit of jetting the tail.}}

'''Trinia''' (trin′i-ä), ''n.'' [NL. (Hoffman, 1814), named after Karl von ''Trinius'' (d. 1844), a botanist of St. Petersburg, and a writer upon grasses.] A genus of umbelliferous plants, of the tribe ''Ammineæ'' and subtribe ''Euammineæ''. {{smaller|It is characterized by flowers with obsolete calyx-lobes, acute petals, and fruit with its ridges traversed by conspicuous oil-tubes. The 7 or 8 species are natives of the Mediterranean region and of temperate parts of Asia. They are smooth branching perennials with decompound leaves, and usually yellow diœcious flowers in compound umbels, with few rays, and few or no bracts and bractlets. For ''T. vulgaris'', see ''honewort''.}}

'''trinidado†''', ''n.'' [So called from the island of ''Trinidad''. See ''tobacco''.] Trinidad tobacco.

{{smaller block|And make the fantastic Englishmen, above the rest, more cunning in the distinction of thy roll ''Trinidado'', leaf, and pudding than the whitest-toothed blackamoor in all Asia.
{{right|''Dekker'' Gull's Hornbook, p. 31.}}}}

{{smaller block|Body o' me! here 's the remainder of seven pound since yesterday — was seven — night. 'Tis your right ''Trinidado''.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man in his Humour, iii. 2.}}}}

'''Trinitarian''' (trin-i-tā′ri-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''Trinity'' + ''-arian''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Pertaining to the Trinity or to Trinitarianism; believing in the Trinity: distinguished from ''Unitarian''. — '''2.''' Pertaining to the order of Trinitarians.

